 does not exist yet; until it does, an update parks the tasks it affects and they
 restart or wait.
 
+### Deciding whether a migration may happen at all
+
+Moving tasks from one distribution to another is decided before anything is written,
+by code that has no way to write. That is the guarantee, not an implementation
+detail: an unsupported migration must leave the project byte-identical, and the
+cheapest way to be certain is for the deciding code to be unable to change anything.
+
+A pair is supported only when all three hold:
+
+- **both digests are recorded**, so it is known what each one consisted of — a
+  receipt naming a digest nobody has a record of is not a receipt;
+- **the target is what the registry serves for that workflow right now**, because
+  planning against anything else describes a migration that cannot be applied;
+- **every step an affected task is sitting on still exists in the target**. A task
+  whose step vanished is not migrated but stranded, and calling that a successful
+  migration would hide a `workflow_missing` park behind a nicer word.
+
+"Affected" is narrower than "pinned to that digest", and each narrowing was a defect
+first. Only OPEN tasks count — a finished one will not run again, so its step must
+neither block a migration nor appear among the tasks being moved. Only tasks of the
+workflow being migrated count: one distribution may register several workflows, so a
+digest alone does not say whose tasks these are, and judging another workflow's task
+by these steps both strands it silently and refuses migrations that never concerned
+it. And an open task whose pin cannot be read stops the plan outright, because the
+reference accounting refuses to forget a distribution while such a task is alive —
+a planner that quietly drove away from it would make two halves of one feature
+disagree about the same task.
+
+The report of which tasks would move is returned whether the answer is yes or no. A
+refusal that does not say which tasks it was about is an answer nobody can act on.
+
+The planner judges exactly the tasks it is handed. It has no way to read the store —
+that is what makes "it cannot write" structural rather than a promise — so supplying a
+complete, reconciled set is the caller's obligation, and a caller that plans around
+tasks it did not show gets an answer to a question nobody asked.
+
+One dead end is worth naming because nothing else names it. A task pinned to a
+workflow the registry no longer serves cannot be migrated — there is no target to
+migrate to — while the reference accounting still counts its digest, so the
+distribution cannot be forgotten either. The task holds both doors shut. The way out
+is the one the identity rule already provides: re-enroll it at a registered
+workflow's first step, which re-pins it onto today's distribution and releases the
+old one, or close it. Neither the planner nor the accounting will do it silently, and
+neither should.
+
+That exit has a precondition worth stating, because it is easy to walk into: enroll
+refuses a task in `work`, and closing refuses while a session is live. An orphan that
+is mid-run has no exit until its session settles. It will settle — the identity check
+parks it — but the operator who tries the remedy first and the daemon that has not
+finished are not looking at the same moment.
+
+Applying a supported migration — idempotent phases, read-back, a receipt with before
+and after hashes — is not implemented. Neither is rollback, and the issue says
+plainly that keeping old blobs does not by itself provide it.
+
